# Cardiac Pathology (Gardner) — 18 image-based items
# stem uses {FIG} where the exhibit goes. choices listed alphabetically; key = index.
Q = [
dict(img='hcm', cap='Opened heart (A) and section of the interventricular septum (B)',
stem="A 17-year-old boy collapses while sprinting during a high school basketball game and cannot be resuscitated. Over the past 6 months he had 2 episodes of lightheadedness during sprints that he did not report. His father died suddenly at age 35 years while jogging. He took no medications and did not use illicit drugs. Toxicology screening at autopsy is negative. The coronary arteries and aorta are normal. Photographs of the opened heart (A) and a microscopic section of the interventricular septum (B) are shown.{FIG}A mutation in the gene encoding which of the following proteins is most likely responsible for this patient's death?",
choices=["Beta-myosin heavy chain","Dystrophin","Fibrillin-1","Potassium channel KCNQ1","Titin"], key=0,
exp="The heart shows massive asymmetric thickening of the interventricular septum that bulges into the left ventricular outflow tract (A), and the septum shows <b>myofibre disarray</b>: huge myocytes running in a swirling, haphazard pattern (B). This is <b>hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</b> (HCM), which affects about 1 in 500 people and is the classic cause of sudden death in young athletes. It is inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern (father died suddenly at 35), and the mutations are in <b>sarcomere genes</b>, most commonly beta-myosin heavy chain (MYH7) and myosin-binding protein C (MYBPC3), also troponin I and T.<br><br>Thick stiff septum → poor diastolic filling + septum interferes with mitral closure → outflow obstruction during exertion → transient ischemia → fatal ventricular arrhythmia. Management: stop strenuous exercise, septal reduction, and an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with myofibre disarray in a young athlete who dies suddenly is hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, caused by autosomal dominant mutations in sarcomere proteins such as beta-myosin heavy chain.",
wrong={1:"Dystrophin mutations (Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophy) cause a <b>dilated</b>, thin-walled heart plus proximal skeletal-muscle weakness. This would fit if the ventricle were big and thin and the boy had progressive weakness.",
2:"Fibrillin-1 mutations (Marfan syndrome) cause sudden death from aortic root dilation, dissection or rupture. The aorta here is normal and the problem is a thick septum.",
3:"KCNQ1 loss of function causes long QT syndrome. It kills through torsades de pointes (a twisting ventricular tachycardia) in a <b>structurally normal</b> heart, so there would be no septal hypertrophy or disarray at autopsy.",
4:"Titin truncating mutations are the commonest known genetic cause of familial <b>dilated</b> cardiomyopathy: a large, thin-walled left ventricle, not a thick septum."},
eli5="Some families pass down a faulty muscle-building instruction, so the wall between the heart's pumping chambers grows far too thick and messy. During hard exercise it blocks blood flow and the heartbeat can go haywire. That is why a young athlete can suddenly collapse."),

dict(img='cband', cap='Section of the anterior left ventricular wall, hematoxylin and eosin stain',
stem="A 61-year-old man comes to the emergency department 2 hours after the onset of crushing substernal chest pain. ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads V1 through V4. Emergency cardiac catheterization shows complete occlusion of the proximal left anterior descending artery, and flow is restored with balloon angioplasty and stent placement. Six hours after the procedure, he develops ventricular fibrillation and cannot be resuscitated. A photomicrograph of the anterior wall of the left ventricle is shown. Neutrophils are not seen.{FIG}Which of the following processes best explains the microscopic findings?",
choices=["Calcium influx into myocytes after reflow","Collagen scar replacing lost myocytes","Myofibre disarray from a sarcomere mutation","Neutrophil enzymes digesting dead myocytes","Persistent ischemia without any restored flow"], key=0,
exp="The myocytes are intensely eosinophilic (pink) and packed with dense, irregular cross-bands. This is <b>contraction band necrosis</b>, the signature of <b>reperfusion injury</b>.<br><br>Ischemic myocyte membranes are leaky → blood flow returns → a flood of calcium enters the damaged cells → hypercontraction of the myofibrils → cross-bands and cell death. Returning oxygen also generates free radicals, which add to the injury. It is seen when flow is restored to a zone within the first few hours, for example after a stent or thrombolysis. The key detail is that flow was restored and the patient died 6 hours later, before neutrophils could arrive.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hypereosinophilic myocytes with dense transverse bands after a stent or thrombolysis are contraction band necrosis, caused by calcium influx into injured myocytes when blood flow returns.",
wrong={1:"A collagen scar is the end stage of an infarct, dense by about 2 months. Death at 6 hours is far too early for any collagen.",
2:"Myofibre disarray is the histology of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, a genetic disease. Here there was an acute coronary occlusion and no thick septum.",
3:"Neutrophils digesting dead muscle is the 1–3 day picture. The stem says neutrophils are absent, and at 6 hours they have barely begun to arrive.",
4:"Ischemia without reperfusion gives quiet coagulative necrosis, with wavy fibres and later loss of nuclei. It does not give dense contraction bands. The flow here was restored."},
eli5="When a blocked heart artery is opened, blood rushes back into muscle that is already hurt. The hurt cells let in too much calcium and squeeze so hard they lock up and die. Under the microscope that shows up as dark pink stripes."),

dict(img='mi2d', cap='Section of the left ventricular wall, hematoxylin and eosin stain',
stem="A 72-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension is found dead at home. Her daughter says that the patient had complained of \"indigestion,\" nausea, and fatigue some days before her death but did not seek care. At autopsy, there is an occlusive thrombus in the left anterior descending artery, and the anterior wall of the left ventricle shows an ill-defined area of discoloration. There is no scar tissue. A photomicrograph of the discolored area is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely interval between the onset of myocardial ischemia and death?",
choices=["30 minutes","6 hours","2 days","10 days","6 weeks"], key=2,
exp="The myocytes have lost their nuclei and cross-striations (coagulative necrosis), and sheets of <b>neutrophils</b> infiltrate between the dead fibres. A brisk neutrophil infiltrate in dead muscle places the infarct at <b>1–3 days</b>.<br><br>The infarct clock: 0–½ hour no light-microscope change → 4–12 hours early coagulative necrosis ± wavy fibres → 12–24 hours early neutrophils → <b>1–3 days brisk neutrophils</b> → 3–7 days macrophages → 7–10 days granulation tissue at the margins → 2–8 weeks collagen → over 2 months dense scar.<br><br>Diabetics and women often have vague symptoms (\"indigestion\"), so infarcts are found late.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Coagulative necrosis with a dense neutrophil infiltrate dates an infarct to 1–3 days.",
wrong={0:"At 30 minutes there are no light-microscope changes, only electron-microscope changes such as mitochondrial swelling.",
1:"At about 6 hours there is at most early coagulative necrosis and edema, with some wavy fibres. Neutrophils are only beginning to arrive by 12–24 hours.",
3:"At 10 days the neutrophils are gone. Macrophages have cleared the dead muscle and granulation tissue with new vessels fills the margins.",
4:"At 6 weeks the infarct is mostly collagen with few cells, and the stem says there is no scar."},
eli5="After heart muscle dies, different clean-up cells arrive on a timetable. Neutrophils, the first responders, crowd in during days 1 to 3. Seeing lots of them tells you the heart attack happened about 2 days earlier."),

dict(img='hemoperi', cap='Opened pericardial sac at autopsy',
stem="A 66-year-old man is admitted to the hospital 20 hours after the onset of substernal chest pain. ECG shows ST-segment elevation with Q waves in leads V2 through V5. He is not a candidate for reperfusion and is treated medically. On hospital day 5, he suddenly becomes confused. His blood pressure is 68/48 mm Hg and pulse is 124/min. The jugular veins are distended to the angle of the jaw, and heart sounds are faint. No new murmur is heard. He had no back pain. He develops pulseless electrical activity and dies. At autopsy, the aortic root and ascending aorta are intact. The opened pericardial sac is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's death?",
choices=["Dissection of the ascending aorta","Interventricular septal rupture","Left ventricular free wall rupture","Papillary muscle rupture","Autoimmune pericarditis"], key=2,
exp="The pericardial sac is filled with dark clotted blood: <b>hemopericardium</b> causing <b>cardiac tamponade</b> (hypotension, distended neck veins, faint heart sounds, then pulseless electrical activity).<br><br>A transmural anterior infarct that was not reperfused → by <b>days 3–7</b> neutrophils and macrophages have digested the dead muscle, but collagen has not yet been laid down → the wall is at its softest → <b>free wall rupture</b> → blood fills the pericardium → the heart cannot fill → death.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Sudden tamponade 3–7 days after a transmural myocardial infarction is left ventricular free wall rupture, occurring when the infarct is softest.",
wrong={0:"Autoimmune (Dressler) pericarditis appears more than 2 weeks after an infarct, with fever, pleuritic pain and a rub. It gives a small inflammatory effusion, not a sac full of clotted blood on day 5.",
1:"Septal rupture happens in the same 3–7 day window, but blood shunts left to right into the right ventricle. It causes a new harsh holosystolic murmur and pulmonary edema, and the pericardium stays empty.",
3:"Papillary muscle rupture (usually after an inferior infarct) causes sudden severe mitral regurgitation, with a new murmur and flash pulmonary edema. There would be no hemopericardium.",
4:"A proximal aortic dissection can bleed into the pericardium, but it presents with tearing chest or back pain, and the aorta here is intact."},
eli5="A few days after a heart attack, the dead patch of wall is as soft as it will ever get. If it tears, blood pours into the sac around the heart and squeezes it so it can't fill. That is what happened here."),

dict(img='boot', cap='Chest x-ray, posteroanterior view',
stem="A 4-year-old boy who recently immigrated with his family is brought to the clinic because of episodes of turning blue during play. During these episodes he stops and squats until the color improves. He is at the 10th percentile for weight. Pulse oximetry on room air shows an oxygen saturation of 82%. There is clubbing of the fingers. A harsh systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border, and the second heart sound is single. Echocardiography shows no patent ductus arteriosus. A chest x-ray is shown.{FIG}The degree of this patient's cyanosis is most directly determined by which of the following?",
choices=["Degree of aortic override","Right ventricular outflow obstruction","Patency of the ductus arteriosus","Size of the ventricular septal defect","Thickness of the right ventricular wall"], key=1,
exp="A \"boot-shaped\" heart (upturned apex from right ventricular hypertrophy and a concave main pulmonary artery segment) in a cyanotic child who squats is <b>tetralogy of Fallot</b>: ventricular septal defect + right ventricular outflow tract stenosis + overriding aorta + right ventricular hypertrophy.<br><br>The ventricular septal defect is large and unrestrictive, so the two ventricles are at equal pressure. Which way blood goes is set by where it meets less resistance: the tighter the <b>right ventricular outflow obstruction</b> → the more deoxygenated blood crosses right-to-left into the aorta → the deeper the cyanosis. Squatting kinks the femoral arteries → raises systemic vascular resistance → pushes blood back toward the lungs, which is why it relieves \"tet spells\".<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In tetralogy of Fallot, the severity of right ventricular outflow tract obstruction sets the size of the right-to-left shunt and therefore how cyanotic the child is.",
wrong={0:"The overriding aorta lets the aorta receive blood from both ventricles, but how much deoxygenated blood actually crosses depends on the outflow obstruction, not on the degree of override.",
2:"A patent ductus can reduce cyanosis in a neonate with severe outflow obstruction by feeding the lungs from the aorta. Echocardiography shows none here, and at age 4 the ductus has closed.",
3:"The defect in tetralogy is typically large and unrestrictive, so its size does not govern the direction or volume of flow. Resistance downstream does.",
4:"Right ventricular hypertrophy is a <b>consequence</b> of pumping against the outflow obstruction. It does not set the shunt."},
eli5="The right side of this heart has a narrow exit to the lungs and a hole into the left side. The narrower that exit is, the more blue, oxygen-poor blood takes the easy route through the hole to the body. So the narrowing decides how blue he gets."),

dict(img='aschoff', cap='Section of myocardium at autopsy, hematoxylin and eosin stain',
stem="A 10-year-old girl living in a rural area with limited access to health care is brought to a clinic because of fever and painful, swollen joints for 2 weeks. The pain began in her knees and then moved to her ankles and wrists. Three weeks before the joint pain began, she had a sore throat that was not treated. Her temperature is 38.4°C (101.1°F) and pulse is 128/min. A holosystolic murmur is heard at the apex. Erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 88 mm/h. Despite treatment, she develops progressive heart failure and dies. A photomicrograph of the myocardium is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the pathogenesis of the lesion shown?",
choices=["Anti-M protein antibodies binding heart antigens","Direct bacterial invasion of the myocardium","Exotoxin blocking protein synthesis in myocytes","Immune complexes lodging in myocardial capillaries","Viral lysis of myocytes with lymphocytic infiltrate"], key=0,
exp="The myocardium contains an <b>Aschoff body</b>: a focus of fibrinoid change surrounded by lymphocytes, plasma cells and activated macrophages with wavy \"caterpillar\" chromatin (Anitschkow cells). It is the lesion of <b>acute rheumatic fever</b>, here with migratory polyarthritis and carditis (a J♥NES picture: Joints, carditis, Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea).<br><br>Untreated group A streptococcal pharyngitis → antibodies and T cells against streptococcal <b>M protein</b> → <b>molecular mimicry</b>: they cross-react with cardiac self-antigens (a type II hypersensitivity reaction) in about 3% of untreated patients → pancarditis. Healing by fibrosis → chronic valve scarring, classically mitral stenosis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Aschoff bodies in acute rheumatic fever result from molecular mimicry: antibodies and T cells against streptococcal M protein cross-react with heart tissue. The bacteria are not in the heart.",
wrong={1:"The heart in rheumatic fever is sterile. The streptococci stayed in the throat weeks earlier. Direct invasion would give neutrophils and abscesses, as in infective endocarditis.",
2:"An exotoxin that blocks protein synthesis (ADP-ribosylation of elongation factor 2) is diphtheria toxin. It causes toxic myocarditis after a grey pharyngeal pseudomembrane, without migratory arthritis.",
3:"Immune-complex (type III) injury drives post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis and the Osler nodes and Roth spots of endocarditis. Rheumatic carditis is a type II reaction against cross-reacting tissue antigens.",
4:"Viral myocarditis gives patchy lymphocytes around injured myocytes after a viral prodrome. It does not produce Aschoff bodies, and the preceding illness here was a strep throat."},
eli5="Her body made antibodies to fight a strep throat. Part of the germ looks like her own heart, so the antibodies attacked her heart by mistake. The germ itself never got into the heart."),

dict(img='ms', cap='Mitral valve viewed from the left atrium at surgery',
stem="A 42-year-old woman who emigrated from Nepal comes to the office because of progressive shortness of breath with exertion for 1 year and an episode of coughing up blood-streaked sputum 2 weeks ago. As a child, she had \"fever and aching joints\" for several weeks. Her pulse is 108/min and irregularly irregular. Crackles are heard at both lung bases. She is referred for surgery, and the mitral valve seen from the left atrium before excision is shown.{FIG}Which of the following findings was most likely present on cardiac auscultation before surgery?",
choices=["Continuous machine-like murmur below the left clavicle","Crescendo-decrescendo murmur at the right upper sternal border","Holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla","Midsystolic click followed by a late systolic murmur","Opening snap followed by a low-pitched diastolic rumble"], key=4,
exp="The leaflets are thick, calcified and fused at the commissures, leaving only a tiny \"fish-mouth\" opening (arrowhead). This is <b>chronic rheumatic mitral stenosis</b>, the late result of acute rheumatic fever in childhood (\"fever and aching joints\").<br><br>Fused, stiff leaflets → the valve snaps open in early diastole (<b>opening snap</b>) → blood squeezes through the narrow orifice → <b>low-pitched diastolic rumble</b> at the apex. A pressure-overloaded left atrium → atrial fibrillation (irregularly irregular pulse) and pulmonary venous congestion → dyspnea and hemoptysis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Rheumatic heart disease causes mitral stenosis (thick leaflets, fused commissures, fish-mouth orifice), heard as an opening snap followed by a diastolic rumble, often with atrial fibrillation.",
wrong={0:"A continuous machine-like murmur is a patent ductus arteriosus. It is a congenital shunt and would not produce the fused, calcified mitral leaflets shown.",
1:"A crescendo-decrescendo murmur at the right upper sternal border is aortic stenosis. It would fit if the lesion were a calcified aortic valve, bicuspid or age-related.",
2:"An apical holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla is mitral <b>regurgitation</b>. That fits a leaky valve, which can also be rheumatic, but this orifice is fixed and pinpoint, the picture of stenosis.",
3:"A midsystolic click with a late systolic murmur is mitral valve prolapse (myxomatous degeneration). Those leaflets are floppy and billowing, not thick and fused."},
eli5="A childhood strep infection scarred her mitral valve so the flaps glued together and left only a tiny slit. Blood has to squeeze through during filling, which makes a snap and then a low rumble. The backed-up left atrium starts to beat irregularly."),

dict(img='amyloid', cap='Endomyocardial biopsy, Congo red stain viewed under polarized light',
stem="A 74-year-old man comes to the office because of progressive shortness of breath with exertion and swelling of both ankles for 8 months. He had surgery for carpal tunnel syndrome in both wrists 5 years ago. His pulse is 88/min and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. The jugular venous pressure is elevated. No murmurs are heard. There is 2+ pitting edema of the ankles. ECG shows low QRS voltage in the limb leads. Echocardiography shows symmetrically thickened left and right ventricular walls, normal-sized cavities, and biatrial enlargement. An endomyocardial biopsy specimen is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes this patient's cardiac dysfunction?",
choices=["Dynamic obstruction of left ventricular outflow by the septum","Impaired diastolic filling of stiff, noncompliant ventricles","Impaired systolic contraction of a dilated, thin-walled ventricle","Right ventricular pressure overload from lung disease","Volume overload from chronic mitral regurgitation"], key=1,
exp="Congo red–stained deposits between the myocytes glow <b>apple-green</b> under polarized light: <b>cardiac amyloidosis</b>. In an older man with prior bilateral carpal tunnel syndrome, this is typically transthyretin (wild-type) amyloid.<br><br>Amyloid infiltrates the interstitium → the walls thicken (but low voltage on ECG, because the added mass is not muscle) → ventricles become stiff → <b>impaired diastolic filling</b> with an often-preserved ejection fraction → high filling pressures → big atria, elevated neck veins, edema. This is the <b>restrictive cardiomyopathy</b> pattern. Its look-alike is constrictive pericarditis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Cardiac amyloidosis is the classic restrictive cardiomyopathy: thick, stiff, nondilated ventricles with impaired diastolic filling, low ECG voltage, and apple-green birefringence on Congo red.",
wrong={0:"Dynamic outflow obstruction is hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy. The thickening there is asymmetric (septal), ECG voltage is high, a systolic murmur is present, and histology shows myofibre disarray.",
2:"A dilated, thin-walled, poorly contracting ventricle is dilated cardiomyopathy. These cavities are normal-sized with thick walls.",
3:"Right ventricular pressure overload from lung disease is cor pulmonale. It thickens only the right ventricle and needs a lung cause. Here both ventricles are involved and the biopsy explains it.",
4:"Chronic mitral regurgitation causes eccentric hypertrophy with a dilated left ventricle and an apical holosystolic murmur. No murmur is heard."},
eli5="Sticky abnormal protein has soaked into the heart walls and made them stiff like cardboard. The heart can still squeeze, but it can't relax and fill properly. Blood backs up into the veins and the legs swell."),

dict(img='chagas', cap='Endomyocardial biopsy, hematoxylin and eosin stain',
stem="A 56-year-old man comes to the office because of progressive shortness of breath with exertion and palpitations for 1 year. He also has difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids. He grew up in a mud-walled house in rural Bolivia and moved to Texas 20 years ago. He has never traveled to Africa. He does not eat pork. ECG shows right bundle branch block and left anterior fascicular block. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle with an apical aneurysm and an ejection fraction of 30%. An endomyocardial biopsy specimen is shown.{FIG}This patient most likely acquired the infection through which of the following routes?",
choices=["Bite of an infected Ixodes tick","Bite of an infected sandfly","Bite of an infected tsetse fly","Ingestion of cysts in undercooked pork","Ingestion of oocysts shed in cat feces","Insect feces rubbed into a bite wound"], key=5,
exp="A myocyte is stuffed with a nest of small round <b>amastigotes</b> (arrow): <b>Trypanosoma cruzi</b>, the cause of <b>Chagas disease</b> (American trypanosomiasis).<br><br>The triatomine (\"kissing\") bug lives in the cracks of mud-walled houses → bites at night and defecates → the person rubs the parasite-laden <b>feces</b> into the bite or the eye → a short, nonspecific acute phase → amastigotes lodge in heart muscle and the nerves of the gut → decades later, in about 20% → dilated cardiomyopathy, conduction disease (right bundle branch block), apical aneurysm, sudden death, and megaesophagus or megacolon (the dysphagia here). The lecture noted Chagas is now considered endemic to the US.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Chronic Chagas disease (dilated cardiomyopathy + conduction block + megaesophagus in someone from rural Latin America) comes from Trypanosoma cruzi, spread when triatomine bug feces contaminate a bite wound.",
wrong={0:"An Ixodes tick transmits Borrelia burgdorferi (Lyme disease), which can cause heart block. It does not cause intracellular amastigote nests, megaesophagus, or a dilated heart decades later.",
1:"A sandfly transmits Leishmania. Amastigotes of visceral leishmaniasis sit in macrophages of the spleen, liver and marrow, not in cardiac myocytes.",
2:"A tsetse fly transmits Trypanosoma brucei (African sleeping sickness), which causes fever and then central nervous system disease. He has never been to Africa.",
3:"Undercooked pork transmits Trichinella, which can cause eosinophilic myocarditis during acute infection, with muscle pain and eosinophilia. He does not eat pork, and trichinosis does not cause megaesophagus.",
4:"Cat feces transmit Toxoplasma, which can also form cysts in myocardium, mainly in immunocompromised patients. It does not explain the Latin American village exposure or the megaesophagus."},
eli5="In some Latin American villages, bugs living in mud walls bite people at night and leave their droppings behind. Scratching rubs a parasite from the droppings into the skin. Years later the parasite has slowly damaged the heart and the gullet."),

dict(img='myxoma', cap='Left atrium opened at autopsy',
stem="A 48-year-old woman is evaluated for 4 months of intermittent fevers, fatigue, and a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss. She also reports several episodes of lightheadedness that occur only when she lies down on her left side. Her temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F). A low-pitched sound is heard in early diastole at the apex. Hemoglobin concentration is 10.6 g/dL, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate is 74 mm/h. Three sets of blood cultures show no growth. She collapses at home and dies before planned echocardiography. The opened left atrium at autopsy is shown.{FIG}Increased production of which of the following substances by the lesion most likely explains this patient's fevers and weight loss?",
choices=["Atrial natriuretic peptide","Erythropoietin","Interleukin-6","Parathyroid hormone–related peptide","Serotonin"], key=2,
exp="A rounded, gelatinous mass hangs in the left atrium (arrow): a <b>left atrial myxoma</b>, the one primary cardiac tumour worth knowing. It is benign, grows near the root of the valve, and histology shows stellate cells in a myxoid stroma.<br><br>Two sets of findings: (1) <b>ball-valve effect</b>: a pedunculated mass flops into the mitral orifice with changes in position → positional lightheadedness or syncope, a diastolic \"tumour plop\", and sudden death; (2) the tumour secretes <b>interleukin-6</b> → constitutional symptoms: fevers, weight loss, anemia, a high erythrocyte sedimentation rate. Negative blood cultures argue against endocarditis, the main look-alike.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Left atrial myxoma causes positional (ball-valve) obstruction of the mitral orifice and constitutional symptoms from interleukin-6 secretion.",
wrong={0:"Atrial natriuretic peptide is released by stretched atrial myocytes and causes natriuresis. It does not cause fever or weight loss.",
1:"Erythropoietin is made ectopically by some tumours (renal cell carcinoma, cerebellar hemangioblastoma) and raises the hemoglobin level. She is anemic.",
3:"Parathyroid hormone–related peptide comes from squamous cell carcinomas and causes hypercalcemia, not fevers.",
4:"Serotonin from a metastatic carcinoid tumour causes flushing and diarrhea and damages the <b>right</b>-sided valves (tricuspid and pulmonic). It does not form a left atrial mass."},
eli5="A jelly-like lump grew inside the heart's upper left chamber. When she lay down it plugged the door between chambers, making her dizzy. It also released a chemical that gave her fevers and made her lose weight."),

dict(img='fibrinous', cap='Heart at autopsy, pericardial surface',
stem="A 67-year-old man is admitted to the hospital 18 hours after the onset of chest pain. ECG shows ST-segment elevation with Q waves in leads V1 through V5, and serum troponin I concentration is markedly increased. He does not undergo reperfusion therapy. On hospital day 3, he develops sharp chest pain that worsens with inspiration and improves when he sits forward. A scratchy, three-component sound is heard over the left sternal border. Serum urea nitrogen concentration is 18 mg/dL. On day 4 he develops ventricular fibrillation and dies. Autopsy shows a transmural infarct of the anterior wall. The pericardial surface is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the pericardial finding?",
choices=["Autoimmune attack on newly exposed cardiac antigens","Blood leaking through a ruptured ventricular wall","Hematogenous spread of Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Inflammation spreading from underlying necrosis","Retention of nitrogenous waste in the blood"], key=3,
exp="The epicardium is coated with shaggy, yellow-tan strands of fibrin, the \"bread-and-butter\" appearance of <b>fibrinous pericarditis</b>.<br><br>Timing tells you which post-infarct pericarditis this is. A <b>transmural</b> infarct reaches the epicardium → acute inflammation spreads from the dead muscle to the overlying pericardium → fibrinous exudate → pleuritic pain relieved by sitting forward and a friction rub, typically <b>2–3 days</b> after the infarct. Dressler syndrome is autoimmune and comes later, beyond 2 weeks.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Fibrinous pericarditis 2–3 days after a transmural myocardial infarction is caused by inflammation extending directly from the necrotic myocardium. Autoimmune Dressler syndrome appears more than 2 weeks later.",
wrong={0:"An autoimmune attack on newly exposed cardiac antigens is Dressler syndrome. It needs time for antibodies to form, so it appears more than 2 weeks after the infarct, not on day 3.",
1:"Blood leaking through a ruptured wall produces hemopericardium: a sac full of clotted blood and tamponade, not fibrin strands. He died of ventricular fibrillation, not tamponade.",
2:"Tuberculous pericarditis is a chronic caseating granulomatous process in an exposed patient, with weeks of fever and weight loss. It would not follow the infarct by 3 days.",
4:"Uremia is a classic cause of fibrinous pericarditis, but his serum urea nitrogen is normal at 18 mg/dL."},
eli5="The heart attack killed muscle all the way through to the outer surface. The inflammation spread to the sac around the heart, which got sticky and rough. That caused the rubbing sound and the sharp pain a few days later."),

dict(img='nutmeg', cap='Cut surface of the liver at autopsy',
stem="A 74-year-old man with a myocardial infarction 6 years ago and a left ventricular ejection fraction of 25% has had worsening shortness of breath, orthopnea, and leg swelling over 4 months. He drinks one glass of wine per week. On examination, there are crackles at both lung bases, jugular venous distention, a smooth enlarged tender liver, and 3+ pitting edema to the knees. Serologic tests for hepatitis B and C are negative. He dies of a ventricular arrhythmia. At autopsy, the lungs are heavy and contain many hemosiderin-laden macrophages. The cut surface of the liver is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the appearance of the liver?",
choices=["Centrilobular congestion from venous backup","Granulomatous inflammation from mycobacteria","Iron deposition within periportal hepatocytes","Macrovesicular steatosis from alcohol metabolism","Periportal hepatocyte necrosis from viral infection"], key=0,
exp="The liver has a mottled red-and-tan cut surface, the \"<b>nutmeg liver</b>\" of chronic passive congestion.<br><br>Failing left ventricle (old infarct, ejection fraction 25%) → back-pressure into the lungs (pulmonary edema, hemosiderin-laden \"heart failure cells\") → pulmonary hypertension → <b>right heart failure</b> → raised systemic venous pressure → blood pools in the central veins and the <b>centrilobular</b> sinusoids (the red dots) around paler, fatty periportal tissue (tan). Prolonged congestion → centrilobular necrosis → fibrosis (cardiac cirrhosis). The commonest cause of right heart failure is left heart failure.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Nutmeg liver is centrilobular congestion from raised hepatic venous pressure, most often right heart failure secondary to left heart failure.",
wrong={1:"Mycobacterial granulomas appear as scattered small white nodules (miliary pattern), not a diffuse red-and-tan network. They would fit a patient with fevers and weight loss.",
2:"Periportal iron is hereditary hemochromatosis. It turns the liver rusty-brown and can cause a cardiomyopathy, but it does not give a red centrilobular pattern.",
3:"Alcoholic macrovesicular steatosis makes a uniformly yellow, greasy, enlarged liver. He drinks very little.",
4:"Viral hepatitis damages hepatocytes through inflammation without a congestive pattern, and his hepatitis serologies are negative."},
eli5="His weak heart couldn't move blood forward, so it backed up into the veins, including the liver's veins. Blood pooled in the middle of each tiny liver unit and made red spots. The cut liver ended up looking like a sliced nutmeg."),

dict(img='dcm', cap='Explanted heart, left ventricle opened',
stem="A 44-year-old woman comes to the office because of progressive shortness of breath, orthopnea, and fatigue for 3 months. Three years ago she was treated for stage II breast cancer that was estrogen receptor–positive and HER2 (human epidermal growth factor receptor 2)–negative. Treatment was surgery, 6 cycles of chemotherapy, and adjuvant hormonal therapy. She does not drink alcohol. A third heart sound and bibasilar crackles are present. Coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries. Echocardiography shows a left ventricular ejection fraction of 20%. Her condition worsens and she undergoes heart transplantation. The explanted heart is shown.{FIG}Which of the following drugs is most likely responsible for this patient's cardiac condition?",
choices=["Bleomycin","Cisplatin","Doxorubicin","Tamoxifen","Trastuzumab"], key=2,
exp="The left ventricle is large and thin-walled (arrow): <b>dilated cardiomyopathy</b>, with systolic dysfunction (ejection fraction under 40%) and normal coronaries.<br><br>Anthracyclines such as doxorubicin, standard in breast cancer chemotherapy, generate free radicals and damage myocytes directly → cumulative, <b>dose-dependent</b> and largely <b>irreversible</b> dilated cardiomyopathy that can appear years after treatment. Hence lifetime dose limits, with dexrazoxane (an iron chelator) as a cardioprotectant. The lecture named cancer therapy as \"the main driver\" of iatrogenic cardiac damage.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Anthracyclines such as doxorubicin cause dose-dependent, irreversible dilated cardiomyopathy that can appear years after chemotherapy.",
wrong={0:"Bleomycin's dose-limiting toxicity is pulmonary fibrosis, not cardiomyopathy.",
1:"Cisplatin causes nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity and peripheral neuropathy, not a dilated heart.",
3:"Tamoxifen, a selective estrogen receptor modulator used as hormonal therapy, raises the risk of venous thromboembolism and endometrial cancer, not cardiomyopathy.",
4:"Trastuzumab (an anti-HER2 antibody) also depresses cardiac function, usually reversibly, but it is given only for HER2-positive tumours. Hers was HER2-negative."},
eli5="One of the strong cancer medicines she had years ago slowly injured her heart muscle. The heart stretched into a big, floppy bag that couldn't squeeze well. This medicine has a lifetime limit for exactly this reason."),

dict(img='lvh', cap='Heart at autopsy, cut in cross-section (scale bar 4 cm)',
stem="An 81-year-old woman with long-standing poorly controlled hypertension, obesity, and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has had exertional shortness of breath and ankle swelling for 2 years. She has no history of myocardial infarction or valve disease. Her blood pressure has ranged from 170/95 to 185/100 mm Hg. She dies of pneumonia. At autopsy, the heart weighs 560 g. A cross-section of the ventricles is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes how sarcomeres were added to her myocytes, her left ventricular ejection fraction, and the main functional defect of her heart before death?",
choices=["In parallel · preserved · impaired filling","In parallel · reduced · impaired contraction","In series · preserved · impaired filling","In series · reduced · impaired contraction","In series · reduced · impaired filling"], key=0,
exp="The left ventricular wall is massively thickened with a small cavity: <b>concentric left ventricular hypertrophy</b>.<br><br>Chronic <b>pressure overload</b> (hypertension) → raised wall stress → myocytes add sarcomeres <b>in parallel</b> → a thicker wall and a smaller cavity → a stiff ventricle that fills poorly → <b>heart failure with preserved ejection fraction</b> (HFpEF, diastolic failure). The lecture's HFpEF profile is an older woman with hypertension, obesity and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, with a stiff titin isoform and perivascular fibrosis.<br><br>Contrast: <b>volume overload</b> (regurgitation, dilated heart after infarction) → sarcomeres added <b>in series</b> → eccentric hypertrophy with a dilated cavity → reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Pressure overload causes concentric hypertrophy (sarcomeres in parallel) and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction from impaired diastolic filling.",
wrong={1:"Sarcomeres in parallel is right, but concentric hypertrophy from hypertension typically keeps the ejection fraction preserved. The problem is filling, not contraction.",
2:"Preserved ejection fraction with impaired filling is right, but adding sarcomeres in series lengthens myocytes and dilates the cavity (eccentric hypertrophy). This cavity is small.",
3:"Sarcomeres in series with reduced ejection fraction and weak contraction is eccentric hypertrophy with HFrEF, as in volume overload or after a large infarct. This heart is thick-walled with a small cavity.",
4:"This combination mixes the eccentric, dilated pattern with a diastolic defect. A dilated, reduced-ejection-fraction ventricle fails mainly because it cannot contract."},
eli5="Years of high blood pressure made her heart pump against a heavy load, so the muscle wall got thick, like a weightlifter's arm. The thick wall still squeezed fine but became too stiff to relax and fill. Blood backed up and she got short of breath."),

dict(img='lqt', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 16-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department after she fainted while swimming in a competitive race. She regained consciousness within 1 minute after being pulled from the pool. She has had 2 similar brief episodes during swim practice in the past year. Her maternal uncle drowned at age 24 years. She takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits, and physical examination shows no abnormalities. Serum potassium, magnesium, and calcium concentrations are within the reference ranges. Echocardiography shows a structurally normal heart. An ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's condition?",
choices=["Gain of function of a cardiac potassium channel","Gain of function of a ryanodine receptor","Loss of function of a cardiac potassium channel","Loss of function of a cardiac sodium channel"], key=2,
exp="The ECG shows a markedly <b>prolonged QT interval</b>. Syncope during exercise, especially swimming, with a young relative's unexplained drowning, normal electrolytes, no QT-prolonging drugs and a structurally normal heart points to <b>congenital long QT syndrome</b>.<br><br>The commonest forms are <b>loss of function of a potassium channel</b>: KCNQ1 (type 1, triggered classically by swimming and exertion) or KCNH2 (type 2, triggered by sudden noise). Fewer outward potassium currents in phase 3 → delayed repolarization → long QT → early afterdepolarizations → <b>torsades de pointes</b> → syncope or sudden death. The lecture's pattern: same gene, opposite direction, opposite disease. Potassium loss gives long QT; potassium gain gives short QT; sodium (SCN5A) gain gives long QT; sodium loss gives Brugada syndrome.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Congenital long QT syndrome is most often caused by loss of function of a cardiac potassium channel (KCNQ1 or KCNH2), which delays repolarization and predisposes to torsades de pointes.",
wrong={0:"Potassium channel gain of function speeds repolarization and causes <b>short</b> QT syndrome. The ECG would show an abnormally short QT, not a long one.",
1:"Ryanodine receptor (RYR2) gain of function causes catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. It also presents as syncope with exertion, but the resting ECG is <b>normal</b>, with no long QT.",
3:"Sodium channel (SCN5A) loss of function causes Brugada syndrome: coved ST elevation in V1–V3 with a right bundle branch block pattern, and events typically at rest or during sleep. It does not prolong the QT."},
eli5="Her heart cells take too long to reset after each beat because a potassium 'exit door' works poorly. During hard exercise the rhythm can spin out of control, and she faints. The ECG shows the slow reset as a long QT."),

dict(img='wpw', cap='12-lead ECG recorded between episodes',
stem="A 23-year-old man comes to the office because of recurrent episodes of a racing heartbeat over the past year. The episodes start and stop suddenly and last 5 to 20 minutes. During one episode, his smartwatch recorded a regular heart rate of 210/min. He does not use stimulants, caffeine supplements, or illicit drugs. His pulse today is 68/min and regular, and physical examination shows no abnormalities. Thyroid-stimulating hormone concentration is normal. An ECG recorded between episodes is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the abnormality on this ECG?",
choices=["Accessory pathway bypassing the atrioventricular node","Conduction block in the right bundle branch","Delayed repolarization of ventricular myocytes","Fibrosis slowing conduction in the atrioventricular node","Reentry circuit confined within the atrioventricular node"], key=0,
exp="At rest the ECG shows a <b>short PR interval</b> (112 ms), a <b>slurred upstroke of the QRS (delta wave)</b> and a slightly wide QRS: ventricular pre-excitation. With episodes of paroxysmal tachycardia this is <b>Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome</b>.<br><br>An <b>accessory pathway</b> (bundle of Kent) links atrium and ventricle directly → part of the ventricle is activated early, skipping the normal atrioventricular nodal delay → short PR + delta wave. The pathway plus the node form a ready-made loop → reentrant supraventricular tachycardia. The lecture lists it among the commonest conduction causes of sudden cardiac death; atrial fibrillation conducted down the pathway can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A short PR interval with a delta wave reflects ventricular pre-excitation through an accessory pathway that bypasses the atrioventricular node (Wolff–Parkinson–White).",
wrong={1:"Right bundle branch block widens the QRS with an rSR′ (\"rabbit ears\") pattern in V1–V2, but the PR interval is normal and there is no delta wave.",
2:"Delayed ventricular repolarization is long QT syndrome. The QT is long, but the PR is normal and the QRS upstroke is not slurred.",
3:"Fibrosis in the atrioventricular node slows conduction and <b>lengthens</b> the PR (first-degree or higher block). This PR is short.",
4:"Atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia is the commonest paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, but between episodes the ECG is normal, with no short PR or delta wave."},
eli5="He was born with an extra electrical wire between the upper and lower heart chambers that skips the usual checkpoint. Some of the signal arrives early, which shows as a ramp at the start of each beat. The signal can also loop round and round, making the heart race."),

dict(img='pfo', cap='Interatrial septum at autopsy, viewed from the right atrium (left) and left atrium (right)',
stem="A 38-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department because of sudden weakness of the right arm and difficulty speaking that began 1 hour ago. Four days ago she returned from a 14-hour flight. She takes an oral contraceptive. Her pulse is 84/min and regular, and she is afebrile. The left calf is swollen and tender, and duplex ultrasonography shows a deep venous thrombosis in the left popliteal vein. ECG shows normal sinus rhythm. Carotid duplex ultrasonography is normal. Blood cultures show no growth. She dies 3 days later of massive cerebral edema. At autopsy, a probe is passed as shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the mechanism of this patient's stroke?",
choices=["Atheroembolus from an ulcerated carotid plaque","Fat embolus from long-bone marrow","Septic embolus from a valve vegetation","Thrombus from a fibrillating left atrium","Venous thrombus crossing the atrial septum"], key=4,
exp="A probe passes from the right atrium to the left atrium under the flap of the septum primum: a <b>patent foramen ovale</b>.<br><br>Long flight + oral contraceptive → leg <b>deep venous thrombosis</b> → a fragment travels to the right atrium → any transient rise in right atrial pressure (coughing, straining, Valsalva) opens the flap → the clot crosses into the left atrium → systemic circulation → middle cerebral artery → stroke. This is <b>paradoxical embolism</b>: a venous clot that ends up in an artery. An atrial septal defect can do the same.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A deep venous thrombosis followed by an arterial (cerebral) embolism means a paradoxical embolus through a patent foramen ovale or atrial septal defect.",
wrong={0:"An atheroembolus from an ulcerated carotid plaque is a common cause of stroke in older patients, but her carotid duplex is normal.",
1:"Fat embolism follows long-bone fractures or orthopedic surgery, with petechiae, confusion and breathing problems. She had no fracture.",
2:"A septic embolus needs endocarditis. She is afebrile and blood cultures are sterile.",
3:"A left atrial thrombus from atrial fibrillation causes the classic embolic stroke of the elderly, but her rhythm is regular sinus."},
eli5="A blood clot formed in her leg after a long flight. Normally it would stop in the lungs, but she had a small flap-door between the two upper heart chambers. The clot slipped through and travelled to her brain."),

dict(img='inferior', cap='12-lead ECG on arrival',
stem="A 64-year-old man comes to the emergency department 1 hour after the onset of substernal chest pressure with nausea and sweating. He has hypertension and hyperlipidemia. His blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg and pulse is 76/min. An ECG obtained on arrival is shown. Thirty minutes later, while awaiting cardiac catheterization, he becomes lightheaded. His pulse is now 38/min, and a rhythm strip shows sinus bradycardia.{FIG}Occlusion of which of the following arteries is most likely responsible for this patient's findings?",
choices=["First diagonal branch","Left anterior descending","Left circumflex","Left main coronary","Right coronary"], key=4,
exp="The ECG shows <b>ST-segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF</b> (the inferior leads, with III greater than II) and reciprocal ST depression in aVL: an <b>acute inferior wall myocardial infarction</b>.<br><br>In most people the inferior wall is supplied by the <b>right coronary artery</b> (right-dominant circulation). The right coronary artery also supplies the <b>sinoatrial node (~60%)</b> and the <b>atrioventricular node (~90%)</b> → ischemia of the conduction system → sinus bradycardia or heart block. It also supplies the posteromedial papillary muscle, which can rupture later.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> An inferior STEMI (ST elevation in II, III, aVF) with bradycardia points to right coronary artery occlusion, because the right coronary artery supplies the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes.",
wrong={0:"A diagonal branch of the left anterior descending artery supplies the anterolateral wall, giving ST elevation in I and aVL, not II, III and aVF.",
1:"Left anterior descending occlusion causes an anterior infarct (ST elevation in V1–V4). It is \"the widowmaker\" and can cause right bundle branch block, not typically sinus bradycardia.",
2:"The left circumflex can supply the inferior wall in a left-dominant heart, but it typically produces lateral changes (I, aVL, V5–V6), and it supplies the sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes far less often.",
3:"Left main occlusion affects both the anterior and lateral walls, usually with cardiogenic shock and widespread ST depression with elevation in aVR, not an isolated inferior pattern."},
eli5="The ECG shows damage on the bottom wall of the heart, which is fed by the right coronary artery. That same artery also feeds the heart's natural pacemaker. When it clogs, the heartbeat slows down too."),
]
